WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.215 --> 00:00:01.090
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Hi.

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:03.940
I'm Cornelius Willis with the
Google Cloud Platform team.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.820
And I'm so excited
today because we

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.880
get to talk to two eminent
technologists at Google.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.060
First, we have Andrew Fikes,
who is a distinguished software

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.740
engineer at Google, who is
working on Bigtable and also

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.930
the Spanner project, which we
published some papers about.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.970
And then Amin Vahdat, who is
Google Fellow and the Technical

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:27.846
Lead for Networking.

00:00:27.846 --> 00:00:29.470
And we brought these
two folks together

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:32.820
because the relationship
between the services we provide

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.590
on Google Cloud Platform and the
capabilities of the underlying

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:38.410
infrastructure are really
what define Google Cloud

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:39.480
Platform as a whole.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.313
You can't really look
at any of these things

00:00:41.313 --> 00:00:43.770
separately and understand
really how they come together

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.070
and provide something
truly extraordinary.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.820
Some questions.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.890
So Amin, you've said in
public talks computing

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.990
is at a crossroads
and networking

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.570
is going to define what
computing is in the future.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.289
So why is that?

00:00:59.289 --> 00:01:00.830
AMIN VAHDAT: That's
a great question.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:02.371
I think it really
is an exciting time

00:01:02.371 --> 00:01:05.190
to be thinking about
the future of computing.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:09.050
We've been to some extent
spoiled in computer science

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.900
for many, many
decades, where we would

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.160
get basically free performance
improvements every 18

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:14.930
months or so.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.430
If you wanted your software to
run faster, all you had to do

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.790
was wait for the latest greatest
processors to become available,

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.550
upgrade your hardware, and
magically your software

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:24.810
runs faster.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.020
For the last few years,
this has changed,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.190
as we've now not
been able to make

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.930
individual processors
faster, but we've

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.400
delivered more of them.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.470
So the programming model
has had the change.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.360
And the network is
really the key enabler

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.140
for bringing more and
more servers together

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.260
to work on bigger
and bigger problems.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.620
So in other words, if I look
at what we do at Google,

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.540
we're bringing together
hundreds, oftentimes thousands,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.590
of servers together on
individual computations.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.530
And it is the
network that allows

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.650
us to treat these hundreds
or thousands of servers

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.180
as one unit of computation.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.680
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
I've heard you say

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.360
that it's important
to have balance

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.090
between the different parts
of the infrastructure,

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.682
and you have to
feed the processors.

00:02:05.682 --> 00:02:06.930
If you have all that
processing capacity,

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.630
you have to make
sure that there's

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:12.190
the network connectivity to
give them data to work on.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.890
AMIN VAHDAT: That's right,
and so one way to look at this

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:18.180
is that servers need
data to compute on.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.780
Just doing computation
without any data

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.520
is not very interesting.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:26.840
And at Google, and I think
at virtually anyplace that's

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:30.304
interesting, the data is
not local to a server.

00:02:30.304 --> 00:02:32.720
There's no way that you're
going to have the data that you

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:33.600
need local to you.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.860
So any data that you're
going to compute on

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.807
is living across the
network somewhere.

00:02:37.807 --> 00:02:39.890
And that's why it's so
great to be working closely

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.580
with folks like Andrew,
because the networking is

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.760
about storage, and I think
storage works very closely

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:46.450
with networking.

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.260
So if you look at
a processor that

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.424
can do billions and billions
of operations every second,

00:02:51.424 --> 00:02:52.840
how do you make
sure that you feed

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.920
it data to keep going
at that rate, otherwise

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.240
it's stalled waiting
for the data.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.115
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So
build our own networking

00:03:00.115 --> 00:03:01.880
gear and our own
networking software.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.184
Why did we start doing that?

00:03:03.184 --> 00:03:04.600
AMIN VAHDAT: A
very simple answer.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.620
We couldn't buy for any price
the scale and performance

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.960
network that we needed
to feed our processors.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.551
In other words, we had
particular applications

00:03:14.551 --> 00:03:16.550
that we needed to run,
starting with web search,

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.520
but continuing across
many different kinds

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.783
of applications, like
today on Google Cloud.

00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:24.050
We couldn't buy a network that
would provide the capabilities

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:24.950
that we needed.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:25.273
CORNELIUS WILLIS: All right.

00:03:25.273 --> 00:03:25.940
Got it.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.866
So you're the Google Lead
for Storage Services--

00:03:28.866 --> 00:03:29.740
ANDREW FIKES: Mm-hmm.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.930
CORNELIUS WILLIS: --how has
your design and the services

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:36.662
you provide different because
of what we have in our network?

00:03:36.662 --> 00:03:38.870
ANDREW FIKES: Well, building
a global storage service

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.810
is, first off,
hard in some ways.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.850
And what Amin and the networking
team brings to the table

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:48.110
is they really take one of those
really difficult problems off

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:51.250
of my deck of things
that I have to deal with.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.370
So oftentimes we're dealing
with extremely large

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:56.060
datasets-- many,
many petabytes--

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.880
and as Amin mentioned, we're
bringing that data together

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.189
to sort of answer
questions and compute over.

00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:04.480
And being able to take the
network out of the equation,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.172
because I can depend
on it to be there,

00:04:06.172 --> 00:04:07.630
I can depend on it
to be really low

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.560
latency, means that I
can make a lot of really

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.336
interesting system
design choices.

00:04:12.336 --> 00:04:14.210
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So it
means that you're not

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:16.462
spending so much time making
sure the network is delivering

00:04:16.462 --> 00:04:16.962
bandwidth.

00:04:16.962 --> 00:04:19.720
You just assume it's there, and
you can design your services

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:20.920
for very, very low latency.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:22.004
Just make that assumption.

00:04:22.004 --> 00:04:23.795
ANDREW FIKES: You can
make that assumption,

00:04:23.795 --> 00:04:26.090
and it also opens up a whole
set of interesting things

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:27.310
that you can do.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:28.830
Amin talked about
how we don't think

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.680
about storage as being local.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.460
That means I can move compute
from one node to another

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.500
very quickly and access it
with the same latencies.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.530
And this lets us work
around hotspots in the data

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:41.020
or machines going down.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.892
It allows us to recover
things much faster.

00:04:42.892 --> 00:04:43.850
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Good.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.490
So how is our-- I know
this is a huge question,

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:50.120
and I'm asking you to
answer it ad hoc like this,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.690
but how is our approach to
networking different from

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.460
everybody else's?

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.210
AMIN VAHDAT: Yeah.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.850
That's a great question,
a hard question.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:00.140
I think it started
with scale and speed.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:04.350
That was the simple part
maybe 10, 12 years ago.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.175
But where we're at
right now is also

00:05:06.175 --> 00:05:11.180
availability and what we
call internal tail latency.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.744
So in other words, when we're
doing distributive computation,

00:05:13.744 --> 00:05:16.160
you don't just care about how
fast you can get at the data

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.570
most of the time-- excuse
me, in the average case.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:21.487
You care about how fast
you can get at the data

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:22.945
at the tail end of
the distribution

00:05:22.945 --> 00:05:24.210
or the 99th percentile.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:24.660
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
So the worst case.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:25.350
AMIN VAHDAT: The worst case.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:26.150
Exactly.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:30.360
So we design for availability,
we design for security,

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.950
and we design for
predictability,

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:34.950
making sure that
the worst case is

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.259
very close to the average case.

00:05:36.259 --> 00:05:37.300
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Got it.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.474
So a very narrow bell curve.

00:05:38.474 --> 00:05:40.529
A very narrow
distribution curve.

00:05:40.529 --> 00:05:43.070
ANDREW FIKES: And that actually
becomes relatively important.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:46.480
If you take a computation which
starts and goes to one server

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.290
and then collects data from
1,000 different servers,

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.880
the probability that you find
that sort of tail latency

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:55.100
and it impacting
your computation

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:56.260
happens all the time.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.770
So you have to really
plan for that worst case.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.849
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So in
distributed operation,

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:04.120
you send it out to
many, many servers.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.650
It comes back and
the last one in--

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:05.880
ANDREW FIKES: Yes.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:07.130
CORNELIUS WILLIS: --is the
one that actually determines

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:07.300
what the performance is.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:08.320
ANDREW FIKES: We have
to wait on all the data

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.452
to come back in order to
deliver a correct answer.

00:06:10.452 --> 00:06:12.910
CORNELIUS WILLIS: The design
that I've heard you talk about

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:19.860
in some of your talks is that
you used all this commodity

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.820
gear and then centrally
control it and use

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.135
sort of centralized
algorithms to determine

00:06:26.135 --> 00:06:29.210
what the actual connectivity
of the network is.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.925
And so that's really different
from the way most people do it,

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:33.425
right?

00:06:33.425 --> 00:06:34.508
AMIN VAHDAT: That's right.

00:06:34.508 --> 00:06:36.620
What's beautiful
about networking,

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.876
as traditionally defined, is
that no one knows their role

00:06:39.876 --> 00:06:40.500
in the network.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.270
Everyone sort of
self-discovers where they are.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:44.020
That's sort of the
basis for the internet.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:46.720
One of the things that we saw
at Google though, is that we're

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:48.250
building these
data centers, we're

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:49.870
building these data
center networks,

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:51.420
and how we connect
all the servers

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.230
together is pre-determined.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.210
In other words, the roles
and responsibilities

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:58.599
of every switching element
is known at design time.

00:06:58.599 --> 00:07:00.890
It's not something that has
to be discovered or learned

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:04.110
over time, as is the case
with the traditional internet.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:07.162
So at Google, again,
without really knowing it,

00:07:07.162 --> 00:07:10.520
we tripped upon the notion of
software-defined networking.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:11.210
Having a--

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:13.210
CORNELIUS WILLIS: And
when was this, by the way?

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:15.830
AMIN VAHDAT: This was about
10 years ago when I started.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:19.140
Now, software-defined networking
has very specific meanings now,

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.740
and it talks about particular
ways of doing things,

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:24.600
but the spirit in
the end is that you

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:28.830
have a logically centralized
control over a very large scale

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:29.762
fabric.

00:07:29.762 --> 00:07:31.765
And that's what
we've wound up at.

00:07:31.765 --> 00:07:35.300
And, frankly, we borrowed these
ideas from storage systems

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:35.990
at Google.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:37.910
We always thought in
terms of-- of course,

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:39.695
Andrew can say much
more about this,

00:07:39.695 --> 00:07:42.630
but a storage infrastructure
consisting of tens of thousands

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:44.600
of disks is
centrally controlled.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:46.110
So it was natural
for us to think

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.090
the same way for how
the network was going

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:49.876
to be planned and controlled.

00:07:49.876 --> 00:07:52.126
CORNELIUS WILLIS: But in the
argument that I've heard,

00:07:52.126 --> 00:07:53.998
and I don't know if
it's a [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:53.998 --> 00:07:56.170
or not but that having
that central control

00:07:56.170 --> 00:08:00.470
also creates a liability,
hence vulnerability.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:02.090
AMIN VAHDAT: It certainly can.

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.940
But what we've found
is that it actually

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:07.864
makes the system simpler in
the end compared to a fully

00:08:07.864 --> 00:08:09.420
decentralized scheme.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.436
You do have the
potential liability

00:08:11.436 --> 00:08:13.060
of a centralized
controller, and that's

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:15.740
why I use the fudge word
logically centralized.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:18.320
We don't literally have
a single point control.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:20.560
It's just that we have
relatively sophisticated

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:23.790
algorithms based on
systems like Chubby

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:26.500
that we've published about in
the past, paxos-based leader

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:29.610
election, that would make it
easy for us to build logically

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.210
centralized control.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.990
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So Google is
running its own private network

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:35.490
connecting all of
its data centers

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:36.490
and all of its services.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:38.820
And if you're a Google
Cloud Platform customer,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.470
you're on the private network.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:43.235
So is it different for
you writing services

00:08:43.235 --> 00:08:45.401
with the understanding that
that's a private network

00:08:45.401 --> 00:08:49.230
rather than being connected
over the public internet?

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.310
ANDREW FIKES: Yes.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:51.893
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
How is it different?

00:08:51.893 --> 00:08:54.940
ANDREW FIKES: I think in many
ways we're fairly spoiled.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:58.090
We have a really strong team
of operations people that

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:00.150
are monitoring this network.

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:02.890
It is the same
network that carries

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:05.410
our user facing traffic
of Search, Gmail,

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:06.130
and other things.

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:10.450
And so it is a resource that
incredibly precious to us.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:12.310
We spend a lot of
time making sure

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:15.400
that the capacity
planning is done well

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:20.500
and that it performs as
sort of as the specs say.

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:22.920
AMIN VAHDAT: The bandwidth
between our data centers

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:24.320
is a real enabler for us.

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:26.860
And, frankly, we have more
bandwidth between our data

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.550
centers than many companies have
inside of their data centers.

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:32.650
And that really
breaks down distance,

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:35.965
where you can think of data
that is across the data center

00:09:35.965 --> 00:09:38.030
as being almost local.

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:39.822
But, frankly, we
can also even think

00:09:39.822 --> 00:09:41.530
of data that's stored
in a different data

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:42.946
center as something
that we can go

00:09:42.946 --> 00:09:45.147
get as part of our
computation in many cases.

00:09:45.147 --> 00:09:47.730
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So I've heard
customers talk about this idea

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:50.404
that they may be
wherever there app is,

00:09:50.404 --> 00:09:52.070
wherever their customer
is in the world,

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:53.580
it's just like the
customer is right there

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:56.061
from a latency and a performance
and customer experience

00:09:56.061 --> 00:09:56.560
perspective.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.518
It doesn't really matter
where they're running.

00:09:58.518 --> 00:09:59.940
ANDREW FIKES: That's right.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:01.400
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
So I understand

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.441
one of the statistics that
has been thrown around

00:10:03.441 --> 00:10:06.530
is that we have bisectional
bandwidth between any two

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:08.924
points in our network of
1 petabyte per second.

00:10:08.924 --> 00:10:14.510
And I also know that the
total aggregate bandwidth

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:17.560
of the entire internet right
now is some fraction of that,

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:20.350
like 2 terabytes per second
or something like that.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:23.570
AMIN VAHDAT: 200 terabytes or
so of the bisection bandwidth,

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:24.250
roughly.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:25.340
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So
what's the right way

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:26.610
to think about
those two systems?

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.651
It's kind of crazy that
Google has more bandwidth

00:10:28.651 --> 00:10:30.530
in its network than the
entire internet does.

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:32.190
AMIN VAHDAT: Well, so
this is the distinction

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:34.522
between local area networks
and the wide area network.

00:10:34.522 --> 00:10:35.980
So one of our data
center buildings

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:39.140
will go scale to about
a petabyte per second.

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:42.950
And that allows all the
servers within that building

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:45.355
in aggregate to talk
to one another at just

00:10:45.355 --> 00:10:46.480
enormous [INAUDIBLE] rates.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.530
So the way to look
at this is that you

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:54.760
can transfer vast, vast, vast
amounts of data among all

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:56.170
the servers to one another.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:57.335
What is this driven by?

00:10:57.335 --> 00:10:59.460
It's the fact that computers
can talk to each other

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:01.510
much faster than people
can talk to the network.

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:05.680
In other words, what drives
wide area network communication

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:06.630
across the internet?

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:10.602
It's people doing web searches,
people consuming videos.

00:11:10.602 --> 00:11:12.310
The amount of bandwidth
required for that

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:14.170
in the grand scheme
of things is tiny

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:16.390
compared to what the servers
within the data center

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:19.122
need to do to provide all
that information to end users.

00:11:19.122 --> 00:11:20.080
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Good.

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:23.960
So when I type in a big
query to a 3 terabyte table,

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.220
and it comes back
in a few seconds,

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.658
it's because of that bandwidth
within that data center.

00:11:28.658 --> 00:11:29.970
AMIN VAHDAT: There's
thousands of servers

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:31.824
that are storing that
data, and if there one

00:11:31.824 --> 00:11:34.824
we wouldn't be able to fit
that data on any one server,

00:11:34.824 --> 00:11:36.240
but even if we
could, we'd run out

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:37.740
of computation power
on that server.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.198
We have to distribute it across
thousands of servers for it

00:11:40.198 --> 00:11:40.698
to work.

00:11:40.698 --> 00:11:42.073
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
So I understand

00:11:42.073 --> 00:11:43.824
we brought a switch
from our data center.

00:11:43.824 --> 00:11:44.990
Can we go take a look at it?

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:45.990
AMIN VAHDAT: Yeah, sure.

00:11:52.384 --> 00:11:54.300
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Wow,
this thing is enormous.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:55.456
What is this?

00:11:55.456 --> 00:11:58.380
AMIN VAHDAT: This is one small
piece of a Jupiter network.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:01.430
This is what powers all the
connectivity in our data center

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:03.240
networks.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:06.640
It is called a super
rack, affectionately

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:09.270
within the group,
and it is one stage

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.370
in a multi-stage
topology that connects

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:12.620
all our servers together.

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:14.670
So this is the middle stage.

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:18.530
This connects all the
server racks to one another

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:20.150
below it at the first stage.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:22.330
And above it is yet
another stage of networking

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:24.590
that looks somewhat like this.

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:27.440
Basically data packets
flow from the servers

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.500
up through the super
rack and then back down

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:31.260
to other servers
across the network.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:33.009
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So
what's different here

00:12:33.009 --> 00:12:35.400
from what I might see
in another data center

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:38.190
that some of our listeners may
have experienced in their data

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:38.700
centers?

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:41.340
AMIN VAHDAT: So when
you buy commercial gear,

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:43.936
you usually buy a
single switch, and it

00:12:43.936 --> 00:12:47.305
might look a lot more glossy.

00:12:47.305 --> 00:12:49.610
It' might have a lot
more shine on it.

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:52.660
It might not have all
of the cables that

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:53.972
are interconnecting things.

00:12:53.972 --> 00:12:55.430
Basically what
we're doing is we're

00:12:55.430 --> 00:12:58.980
exposing the fact that
any big switch is composed

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:00.500
of a lot of small ones.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:03.370
So if you were to zoom in
here what you would notice

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:06.600
is that right over here
is actually one switch.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.830
It's about 7 inches tall,
and there are eight of them

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:13.230
in this column,
another eight in here.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:16.700
So a total of 16 switches
in this super rack.

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:19.130
Each one of these switches
is a commodity switch.

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:22.130
So the things that
anyone might buy

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:25.570
is a standard top of rack
switch on the commodity,

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.315
and they're driven by volume.

00:13:27.315 --> 00:13:28.690
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
And so I notice

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:33.560
that we got yellow fiberoptic
cables here but also a lot

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:35.107
of conventional copper as well.

00:13:35.107 --> 00:13:36.190
AMIN VAHDAT: That's right.

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:38.200
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So how
do those work together?

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:39.700
What's the reason we have that?

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:42.640
AMIN VAHDAT: So we
have some black cables.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:43.900
These are the copper cables.

00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:46.350
And again, in
traditional networks,

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:48.517
which is what you would
have, is a copper backplane.

00:13:48.517 --> 00:13:49.308
It would be hidden.

00:13:49.308 --> 00:13:50.320
It would be in the back.

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:52.680
It would have copper traces
etched on a piece of metal.

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:54.138
And that would
provide connectivity

00:13:54.138 --> 00:13:57.940
among all of the individual
switching elements.

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:01.640
Here we've chosen to expose that
by using commodities switches.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.780
Again, commodity switches
don't have the big backplane.

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:08.140
So we actually provide
connectivity from every switch

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:10.120
to every other switch
within the super rack.

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:12.020
And those are the
black copper cables.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:14.800
Copper cables have
a limited reach,

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:18.230
about the reach of
this super rack here.

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:21.080
If you want to leave the
super rack, distances

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:24.690
beyond a few meters, you
need to go fiber optics.

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:26.480
So these yellow
interconnects are

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:29.310
the interconnects that leave
the super rack, either going

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:32.220
to the higher stage of
the switching topology

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:34.007
or down to the server racks.

00:14:34.007 --> 00:14:35.590
So for example, the
server racks would

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:38.940
have also the other
end of the yellow fiber

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:41.540
optic cables that connect
to the copper rack switch.

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:43.290
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So
how is this different

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:45.670
from earlier generations?

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:48.670
AMIN VAHDAT: We
actually went along

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:51.460
a number of different lines
in building our switching

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:53.432
infrastructure for modularity.

00:14:53.432 --> 00:14:55.290
And the software
and the hardware

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:58.320
is designed for being able
to support upgrade in place

00:14:58.320 --> 00:14:59.540
across the data center.

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:02.160
And that was really
a key insight

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:05.120
in that to be able to
maintain availability

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:07.030
of our infrastructure,
we had to live

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:09.160
with upgrading it continuously.

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:11.166
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So the
Google Cloud Platform

00:15:11.166 --> 00:15:13.040
that our customers are
on today is constantly

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:15.427
being upgraded and revised.

00:15:15.427 --> 00:15:16.510
AMIN VAHDAT: That's right.

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:18.790
This is just one
generation of our switching

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:20.098
gear, one of many.

00:15:20.098 --> 00:15:24.850
And what we found is that both
internally for Google products

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:27.210
from search to YouTube,
no one can only

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:30.290
tolerate taking down an entire
data center for six months

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:32.620
while we upgrade the
entire infrastructure.

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:34.600
What that means is
that all of our servers

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:37.320
must continuously run
while the network is

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.590
being upgraded from one
generation to the next.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:41.830
And this super rack
is a modular piece.

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:44.170
It is interchangeable
with other super racks

00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:48.081
such that we can be upgrading
one for another with no service

00:15:48.081 --> 00:15:48.580
downtime.

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:49.881
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So
this is not the be all

00:15:49.881 --> 00:15:51.242
and end all of Good networking.

00:15:51.242 --> 00:15:52.450
AMIN VAHDAT: Oh, far from it.

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:56.040
So this was designed and
deployed in about 2012.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:59.350
It was the fifth generation
of in-house Google networking

00:15:59.350 --> 00:15:59.890
gear.

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:01.700
You can imagine that
since 2012 we've

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:04.380
been working on subsequent
generations, some of which

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:06.720
have seen deployment
in the data center now.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:10.645
And as we speak, we're designing
the next next generation

00:16:10.645 --> 00:16:12.260
of hardware and
software that will

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:14.140
go into the data
center for deployment

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:17.290
in 2016, 2017, and 2018.

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:19.990
It's never ending to deliver
the latest and greatest

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:21.310
for Google's network.

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:22.730
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So Andrew,
a lot of atoms in one

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:24.271
place here for a
software guy, right?

00:16:24.271 --> 00:16:26.090
ANDREW FIKES: Yes, absolutely.

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:28.491
It's quite professional looking.

00:16:28.491 --> 00:16:30.866
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So let's go
talk more about the future.

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:41.730
So that is quite a machine.

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:44.900
AMIN VAHDAT: It's some switch.

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:46.330
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
So what is what

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.375
we're doing there is
unorthodox or controversial?

00:16:48.375 --> 00:16:51.560
How have some of the designs
we've put out there influenced

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:52.830
the industry?

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:55.370
AMIN VAHDAT: Well, that's a
very interesting question.

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:57.250
I think when we
started down this path,

00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:59.770
many people thought that
it just couldn't work.

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:00.430
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
Commodity switches.

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:02.221
AMIN VAHDAT: Commodity
switches, delivering

00:17:02.221 --> 00:17:04.910
the right levels of
availability, the right levels

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:05.589
of performance.

00:17:05.589 --> 00:17:07.389
But we thought it inevitable.

00:17:07.389 --> 00:17:09.680
In other words, if you look
at the history of computing

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:12.380
and the history of storage,
it's followed similar trends.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:16.121
Network has just gone down
that path most recently.

00:17:16.121 --> 00:17:17.390
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Got it.

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:21.869
So how are apps going to
change and programming

00:17:21.869 --> 00:17:23.056
models going to change?

00:17:23.056 --> 00:17:25.180
What does this mean further
up the stack for people

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:26.359
that are creating software?

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:27.150
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah.

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:30.120
So I mentioned earlier
sort of as a programmer,

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:33.730
as a system developer, being
able to depend on the network

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:35.902
to get access to my data.

00:17:35.902 --> 00:17:37.810
And I think what we've
seen historically

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:43.580
is steps to sort of disaggregate
compute from data itself.

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:47.370
So this started with creating
disks that were remote

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:49.350
from where our servers were.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:50.910
We've been looking
at disaggregating

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:53.950
other types of storage
recently, be it flash

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:56.490
or upcoming in [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:58.940
And so what I think
you'll see going forward

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:00.920
is much more of this
programming model

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:06.200
changes around being able to
ask other nodes or other pieces

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:08.790
of compute very quickly
over the network

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:11.074
to retrieve data and sort
of bring it all together.

00:18:11.074 --> 00:18:12.740
And so from a programming
point of view,

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:16.010
it'll be as if your data
is local no matter where

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:18.220
your compute happens to be.

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:21.280
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Wow, so it's
taking the program wherever

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:22.162
the data is almost.

00:18:22.162 --> 00:18:24.620
ANDREW FIKES: It's taking the
program wherever the data is.

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:28.962
It also lets us sort of
coordinate more effectively.

00:18:28.962 --> 00:18:31.170
So when you're thinking
about large-scale distributed

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:34.060
computation, you have a lot
of tradeoffs you can make.

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:36.547
You might find that one
server is slower than another.

00:18:36.547 --> 00:18:38.880
And you have the ability to
go out and ask people really

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:40.730
quickly and answer questions.

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:43.120
And sort of super-fast
networks that you can depend on

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:46.070
with low latency
frees you up to try

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:47.979
some of these more
interesting techniques.

00:18:47.979 --> 00:18:50.520
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So the thing
that occurs to me you as we're

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:54.420
talking about this is that the
same kind of disaggregation

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:56.830
that you're doing
in the network space

00:18:56.830 --> 00:19:00.746
follows what we did in the
compute space as well, in that

00:19:00.746 --> 00:19:02.990
rather than think
about the data center

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:05.280
as a collection of
nodes, we think about it

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:06.190
as one big computer.

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:07.273
AMIN VAHDAT: That's right.

00:19:07.273 --> 00:19:09.220
CORNELIUS WILLIS: And
networking and storage

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:10.680
and even the
distributive services

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:12.263
that run on our
infrastructure are all

00:19:12.263 --> 00:19:14.660
kind of designed with that
same core idea in mind.

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:18.845
It's the warehouse scale
computer as the paper says.

00:19:18.845 --> 00:19:20.220
AMIN VAHDAT: Really
is the vision

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:22.803
that we're driving after is that
you can think of the building

00:19:22.803 --> 00:19:25.620
as one unit of compute,
one unit of storage

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:27.930
and one large switch that
interconnects all of them

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:29.010
together.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:30.464
And as Andrew was
mentioning, you

00:19:30.464 --> 00:19:32.630
don't need to think about
where your data is stored.

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:33.890
It's available to you always.

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:36.685
It's available to you really
quickly, and in the end,

00:19:36.685 --> 00:19:40.310
convenient programming is
being able to just reference

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:42.262
your data, compute
on it, generate

00:19:42.262 --> 00:19:43.220
the next piece of data.

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:46.580
CORNELIUS WILLIS: When you
think about the day in the life

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:50.899
of the average cloud computer--
cloud developer-- today,

00:19:50.899 --> 00:19:53.350
more than half their
time is spent just trying

00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:55.576
to make all this stuff work.

00:19:55.576 --> 00:19:57.284
And if you take that
out of the equation,

00:19:57.284 --> 00:19:59.857
think about what amazing
apps people can write.

00:19:59.857 --> 00:20:00.940
ANDREW FIKES: That's true.

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:03.680
We spend a lot of time
doing integration work,

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:06.190
so bringing pieces of
those things together.

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:08.190
And at Google we've
started to think a lot

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:11.570
about how programming models
can change to sort of work

00:20:11.570 --> 00:20:13.280
around these
new-found latencies,

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:15.670
these new access-related
things, and how that actually

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:20.020
changes how people
develop applications.

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:22.380
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So
what's next for your teams?

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:24.620
What should we expect?

00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:26.707
AMIN VAHDAT: You know,
it's a never-ending list

00:20:26.707 --> 00:20:27.290
of challenges.

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:30.279
So I think certainly on the
networking side, of course,

00:20:30.279 --> 00:20:31.820
the networks are
going to get faster.

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:34.060
They're going to
get lower latency.

00:20:34.060 --> 00:20:36.780
I think that for us, one of the
really interesting challenges

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:41.260
is how do we expose this all
the way to the Google Cloud

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:42.030
customer.

00:20:42.030 --> 00:20:45.300
In other words, we have a
fairly specialized and powerful

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:48.290
programming [INAUDIBLE] that
we can deal with in networking

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:49.220
environments.

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:52.420
How do we securely and
safely expose access

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:55.620
to basically, if you will,
bare metal performance

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:59.000
all the way to the
virtualized customers.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:01.482
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
Productizing inside of Google.

00:21:01.482 --> 00:21:03.617
How about for your team, Andrew?

00:21:03.617 --> 00:21:05.200
ANDREW FIKES: We've
been doing storage

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:07.370
at Google for a long time.

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:10.580
I'm actually very, very excited
about our cloud customer

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:11.450
workloads.

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:14.350
They bring in new
challenges for us.

00:21:14.350 --> 00:21:16.910
And they give us an opportunity
to solve some really

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:19.160
interesting problems.

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:23.499
You know, Amin mentioned earlier
that our switches are developed

00:21:23.499 --> 00:21:25.790
and our networks are developed
years in advance, right?

00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:28.050
It's a continually
upgrade process.

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:30.780
And so from a storage
development point of view,

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:33.940
we're already starting to plan
for what the next networks will

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:38.990
bring us, what capabilities
it will actually unlock.

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:40.890
And so for us, we're
starting to think

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:43.450
about new tiers of storage
and how we integrate those

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:46.000
into applications and
how they can actually,

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.990
as Amin said, impact
cloud user experiences,

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:50.940
how we can make
development simpler,

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:53.630
and how we can make
reliability, availability,

00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:56.722
and consistency of operations
around storage be better.

00:21:56.722 --> 00:21:58.430
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So
it's exciting times.

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:00.596
This is the right time to
be working on Google Cloud

00:22:00.596 --> 00:22:02.390
Platform.

00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:04.070
Thank you both.

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:08.890
Thank you all for watching
today, and go out and try it.

